        {
          "path": "tests/test_skill_scorecard.py",
          "issue": "Spec's '## existing tests' table marks this file (and tests/test_spawn.py, tests/test_promotion.py) as 'missing', but all three already exist with real passing suites (test_skill_scorecard.py has SkillScorecardTests with test_use_rate_and_overhead_ratio_per_skill and test_skill_tokens_per_accepted_task). tests-green.sh runs the whole tests/ directory via unittest discover, not just the acceptance-named ids, but it also does not assert that pre-existing test methods remain present -- so an executor that trusts the stale 'missing' label and recreates/overwrites the file instead of appending a method to the existing TestCase class would silently delete existing coverage and the gate would not catch it.",
          "severity": "med",
          "line": 0
        },
        {
          "path": "orchestrator/skill_router.py",
          "issue": "skill_router.select()'s 'tokens_exposed_l0' (used for skill_reduction = 1 - tokens_selected_l0/tokens_exposed_l0 in step 5) is not pinned to a specific baseline. The existing stage-1 exposure computed in spawn.py's _skill_exposure and executor.py's _codex_skill_meta sums tokens across ALL active builtin registry skills regardless of role (matching what the shared .claude/skills directory actually shows the worker), whereas step 2 defines candidates as 'registry ids for the role' (a much smaller, role-filtered set). Depending on which figure the router reports as tokens_exposed_l0, skill_reduction will differ by roughly an order of magnitude and mean different things (true context savings vs. role-relevance savings). No listed acceptance test pins the exact value, so this ambiguity won't fail a test, but it will produce an economy metric whose meaning is unclear when Stage 5 promotion decisions later read it.",
          "severity": "low",
          "line": 0
        }
      ]
    },
